WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.970
The 2007 film Zodiac is based on the mysterious
California criminal in the 60s and 70s, but

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.640
interestingly leaves certain crime scenes
out of the movie, like the first incident,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.350
which took place in December of 1968 on Lake
Herman Road in Benicia.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:17.000
The movie simply opens with the second confirmed
case, the July 4th 1969 ordeal in Blue Rock

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.779
Springs, but in each crime scene that follows,
we only see certain details about the Zodiac.

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:25.580
This isn't just done to build suspense and
make the movie scary; there is a specific

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:29.199
reason that director David Fincher held back
information the way he did.

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:33.270
This was the director's first foray into non-fiction,
and given the ongoing nature of the case,

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.330
he wanted to make the movie as accurate possible.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:39.400
So the motivation for every Zodiac appearance
was to base it off of the testimony of surviving

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.040
witnesses.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.860
The Lake Herman Road shooting had no survivors,
and so, was mentioned, but never shown in

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:44.870
the movie.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.990
The Blue Rock Springs affair had one survivor,
and the Zodiac is shown covered in shadow

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:52.170
with a light shining in our faces to fit the
circumstances of that July 4th night.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.900
The Lake Berryessa stabbing in September of
that year also had a survivor, and we see

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.930
the Zodiac dressed up to hide his identity, as
described by the living victim.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:03.260
And the Presidio Heights occurrence doesn't
give the viewer any information that the kids

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.240
who called in the felony wouldn't have been
able to see for themselves.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.939
This was done to ensure that the filmmakers
were sticking to the truth.

00:01:08.939 --> 00:01:12.869
The first confirmed victims were a couple
who both died in 1968, so the filmmakers don't

00:01:12.869 --> 00:01:16.609
really have an account of the events, so in
order to create the scene on film, they would

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:17.609
have to make stuff up.

00:01:17.609 --> 00:01:21.610
To see other examples of the painstaking dedication
to accuracy, and the meaning behind some of

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:25.160
the many posters that appear, stick around
to the end of this video.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:30.860
Welcome to Things You Missed.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:34.030
I believe this is the first non-fiction episode
of Things You Missed.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:38.090
The only partial exception would be The Conjuring
franchise movies, which are still fictional

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:40.469
stories, but they are based on real life characters.

00:01:40.469 --> 00:01:44.500
So far Zodiac is the only movie I've covered
that adapts only real life events.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.840
The obvious reason, is that this is a horror
channel, and it's often considered rude to

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:53.630
turn someone's real life horrors into popcorn
cinema, but Zodiac does an excellent job of

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.680
being accurate, respectful, and still having
kills that very suspenseful and scary.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.620
"Not many people have basements in California..."

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.040
"I do."

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.780
In fact it does much better than many fictional
horror movies.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:12.069
I'm not gonna name any names.

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:14.069
Not gonna name The Turning.

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:16.140
Not gonna name Black Christmas 2019.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.100
Not gonna name Countdown.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.640
Definitely not gonna name Brightburn...

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:29.090
The depictions of the Zodiac weren't the only
aspects where extreme attention to accuracy

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:29.820
was paid.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:34.120
The opening logos of the film are reminiscent
of the logos used by those studios during the

00:02:34.129 --> 00:02:37.430
60s, complete with dust and scratches on the
film to make it feel as if we're watching

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.240
an old movie.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.040
The wardrobe department used all available
information to recreate what each victim was

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:46.530
wearing, and I quickly figured this out throughout
the movie, all the newspapers seen on camera

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.250
are real prints from the Zodiac investigation.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:53.740
"In the far corner, there's a stack of the
day's newspapers for the San Francisco Chronicle,

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.380
and there is in the movie too.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:59.820
You could walk onto the set, pick up the
third newspaper from a stack of twenty at

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.319
the bottom, open up to page 37 and read the
article that was in the Chronicle that day.”

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:08.980
The production had access to information that
the general public did not, so it's not always

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.340
obvious how much care was taken to get these
things right, but one scene where it was obvious

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:17.640
was the infamous Melvin Belli broadcast, where the
Zodiac demanded that the prominent attorney

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.409
appear on the talk show A.M.

00:03:19.409 --> 00:03:20.440
San Francisco.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:24.069
Fincher's approach was not just to make an
entertaining movie, but also to create a competent

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:25.209
piece of journalism.

00:03:25.209 --> 00:03:28.549
After reading the script, which is based on
the main character Robert Graysmith’s book,

00:03:28.549 --> 00:03:32.480
which we actually see on shelves in the latter
half of the movie, Fincher wanted to go even

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.510
deeper and meet the surviving figures in the
investigation for the purposes of maintaining

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.900
authenticity and journalistic integrity.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:43.020
In a way, the movie, in its pursuit of getting
all the little details right, became the ultimate

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.370
knowledge database for details about the case.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:48.760
As we see in the film, part of the obstacle
that prevented the case from being solved

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:52.129
was that you had several different police
departments with jurisdiction over certain

00:03:52.129 --> 00:03:56.260
areas, who didn't always communicate and share
the info with each other as well as they could

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:56.760
have.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.200
This next one isn't necessarily a Thing You
Missed in the movie, but it's definitely something

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.849
that you wouldn't know if you had just watched
the movie.

00:04:02.849 --> 00:04:04.450
We see at the end, the aftermath.

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.880
"In 2004, the San Francisco Police Department
deactivated their Zodiac investigation."

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:16.020
The movie Zodiac was released on March 2nd , 2007 and that same month, the case was reopened in San Francisco, where it

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:17.420
remains open today.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:21.150
If you’re curious, the biggest update came
in 2018, where some of the letters sent in

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:24.520
to the newspapers were sent to a lab, where
it was thought that some new forensic testing

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:29.180
could maybe match DNA from the back of the
stamps, however, as of 2020, still no results have

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.199
been reported.

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:35.000
I do find it somewhat ironic that a movie
would be what theoretically reopened the case

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.880
in San Francisco, because one of the theories
about the Zodiac was that he was a big fan

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.600
of movies and pop culture, leading the detectives
at one point to go after a suspect named Rick

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.650
Marshall, who worked at movie theaters on
two separate occasions.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:49.740
“The Zodiac Killer is obsessed with movies.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:51.920
He recorded his murders on film.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:55.500
I tried to tell the police but they wouldn’t
follow through on it.”

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:56.640
What?

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.080
What, no, no I wasn’t alive in the 60s.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:01.960
I SWEAR.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.199
It is at one point suggested that the Zodiac
symbol could be related to the crosshair imagery

00:05:06.199 --> 00:05:10.340
seen in a film leader, and Graysmith makes
a connection in which one of Zodiac’s cyphers

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:14.180
appears to reference a film called The Most
Dangerous Game in which a hunter goes after

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.360
humans for sport.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.770
Zodiac is also rich with cinema references,
most notably to one 1971 action flick called

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:21.780
Dirty Harry.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.940
“This is about a movie about a couple of
killers.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.160
Harry Callahan.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.160
And a homicidal maniac.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:34.400
The one with the badge is Harry.”

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:38.640
It’s loosely based of the Zodiac murders,
with an antagonist named Scorpio, which is

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:41.759
one of the twelve Zodiac signs, who sends
threats to the San Francisco Chronicle.

00:05:41.759 --> 00:05:43.900
There are so many connections here.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:46.650
I hope the use of a crosshair symbol was not
lost on you.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:51.139
Dirty Harry also takes place in San Francisco
and in a Zodiac featurette, the screenwriter,

00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:55.419
James Vanderbilt, suggests that Dirty Harry
was actually based on Detective Dave Toschi,

00:05:55.419 --> 00:05:58.310
the real life version of the Mark Ruffalo
character.

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:04.810
“Mark was able to bring that wonderful humanity
that you don’t expect to find in the guy

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:08.470
who Bullitt was based on, and Dirty Harry
was based on...”

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:12.540
And the diner where Toschi likes to eat in
Zodiac is called Callahan’s diner.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:13.920
“Harry Callahan.”

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.460
The movie that Toschi goes to see, where he
first meets Robert Graysmith?

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:18.460
Dirty Harry.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.610
A poster can be seen as he exits the cinema.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:24.590
Then, at the last scene that Graysmith and
Toschi share together in Callahan’s diner,

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:25.980
there’s this line.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:30.820
“Just cause you can’t prove it doesn’t
mean it’s not true.”

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:32.220
“Easy Dirty Harry.”

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:36.780
There would be even more significant movie
references in Zodiac, that the Zodiac himself

00:06:36.789 --> 00:06:40.900
would probably be proud of, but those wouldn’t
be the only connections and Things You Missed

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:42.120
yet to come.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.160
In Robert Graysmith’s book, he describes
the crimes in great detail, but doesn’t

00:06:55.169 --> 00:06:59.250
really talk about his personal life or the
reason he becomes so obsessed with the case.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:02.520
In the movie, we see the sad evolution of
his personal life, and if you’re paying

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:07.470
close attention, there is a suggestion about
why he becomes so fascinated with Zodiac.

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:11.620
It seems that Graysmith, or at least the movie
version of Graysmith, may have been a crime

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:12.620
movie buff.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:15.690
Here we see a poster in his living room for
Alfred Hitchcock’s The Wrong Man.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.710
Nearby is a poster of John Huston’s The
Asphalt Jungle.

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:22.850
Behind the couch is a poster for the 1955 crime
flick, I Died A Thousand Times.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:26.380
By the door, he’s got a poster for the 1955
movie, Illegal.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:30.009
As we know, one of Graysmith’s strongest
assets isn’t his movie knowledge, but his

00:07:30.009 --> 00:07:31.180
knowledge of books.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:35.590
By the phone is a series of book covers, for
the original James Bond stories by Ian Fleming,

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:39.889
which I realize may fall more under action,
but they definitely still have aspects of the crime

00:07:39.889 --> 00:07:40.480
genre.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.380
At one point, Robert suggests the idea of
tracking books that Zodiac may have used to

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.600
develop the code used in his cyphers.

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:51.139
“Now I started thinking that if you can
track these books, then maybe you can track

00:07:51.139 --> 00:07:52.229
the man.”

00:07:52.229 --> 00:07:56.430
We’ve seen this tactic used once before
in a crime movie directed by David Fincher,

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:00.530
and the crime I’m referring to is not stealing
the idea for an $800B website.

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:05.520
I’m talking about Se7en, where we see Detective
Somerset who similarly tries to solve a themed

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.340
serial killer case by using library records.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:09.840
“Ask yourself.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:13.400
What would he study to do the things he’s
done?

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:15.040
What was that just now?

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:18.200
Jack the Ripper, for instance.”

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:19.500
“Where are we going?”

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:20.500
“Library.”

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:25.000
Obviously, that came out before Zodiac, but
Zodiac is based on events that happened before

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:25.860
Se7en.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:30.560
So I wonder if Fincher was already an enthusiast
of the Zodiac case before he made Se7en, than

00:08:30.569 --> 00:08:33.169
that’s what inspired him to put in that
plot point.

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:36.840
Supposedly, Fincher was the first name they
went to about directing Zodiac, because he

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.289
had already made the definitive serial killer
film.

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:41.919
There’s also a possible reference to another
Fincher film.

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:43.329
A much sillier one.

00:08:43.329 --> 00:08:44.640
Let’s roll the clip.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:49.660
“Now a question of etiquette.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:52.280
As I pass, do I give you the ass or the crotch?”

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:00.940
This can’t be a coincidence right?

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:01.960
“Excuse me.”

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:02.940
“Dave.”

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:03.800
It CAN’T.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:04.300
BE.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:05.160
A coincidence…

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.899
Well, there's one thing that’s definitely not a
coincidence in this.

00:09:08.899 --> 00:09:12.279
In the background of this scene at the police
station, there’s a cameo appearance from

00:09:12.279 --> 00:09:16.470
Bryan C Hartnell, the real life survivor of
the Lake Berryessa attack, who can be seen

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:18.900
as an adult in the documentary, This Is The
Zodiac Speaking.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:23.911
Another possible pattern in the Zodiac case
that gets brought up in the film and the book

00:09:23.911 --> 00:09:26.769
is that each of the attacks that Zodiac has
proven his involvement in have some kind of

00:09:26.769 --> 00:09:28.560
connection to a body of water.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.520
LAKE Herman Road, Blue Rock SPRINGS, LAKE
Berryessa, which was actually a reservoir,

00:09:33.529 --> 00:09:38.290
but still water, and then WASHington and Cherry,
the weakest example of the bunch.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:42.560
So there are also aquatic references in the
movie, like Graysmith’s favorite adult beverage

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:46.120
being something called an Aqua Velva, the
first shot of the film looking out over the

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:51.380
water and a poster in the movie theater organist’s
house for Key Largo, a crime / noir movie

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:53.700
set next to the ocean in Key Largo, Florida.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:57.180
As I mentioned before, one of the frustrating
parts of this case was that years and years

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:01.280
would go by where nothing new would happen,
and I think this stagnation is symbolized

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:05.350
perfectly when Graysmith eventually goes to
visit his old pal, Paul Avery and finds him

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:07.139
in the middle of a game of Pong.

00:10:07.139 --> 00:10:10.970
Instead of pausing it, the game continues
to run, with neither side making a move the

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:12.019
entire time.

00:10:12.019 --> 00:10:16.860
The film’s ending, like the real case, doesn’t
offer closure to the plot, but I find the

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:19.540
film does give closure for a couple of the
characters.

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:23.310
Graysmith at one point tells his wife that
it’ll be over when he’s able to look Zodiac

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:25.089
in the eye and know that it’s him.

00:10:25.089 --> 00:10:28.500
In the book, the hardware store scene is a
mission to try to gather more information

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:32.480
about Arthur Leigh Allen, primarily to get
a real handwriting sample when he

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:36.570
doesn’t suspect it, but in the movie all
we see is the eye contact between Graysmith

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:42.600
and Allen; the smile slowly disappearing from
his face as he realizes who this visitor is.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:46.839
The very last scene provides closure for another
character, and it brings the entire movie

00:10:46.839 --> 00:10:47.860
full circle.

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:52.230
The first character to appear on screen was
Mike Mageau, and 22 years later after pointing

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:55.959
out the man that forever changed his life,
he would be the last character to appear,

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:57.640
before a cut to black.

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:01.080
The end credits music is the same music that they listened to on the radio that night.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:05.779
While we may never know for sure who the Zodiac
killer was, this scene tells us that for the

00:11:05.779 --> 00:11:09.139
oldest confirmed survivor, the case was closed.

00:11:09.139 --> 00:11:13.290
But as one case closes, another one opens
here on Things You Missed because in the next

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:17.310
episode I’ll be wrapping up my series of
David Fincher analysis’ with my personal

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:19.930
favorite, sorry Zuck, it’s Gone Girl.

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:24.660
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week, ring the deathbell for

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:27.589
notifications and I’ll see you in the next
one.

00:11:27.589 --> 00:11:30.320
Assuming we stay inside.

